*By Lev Romanyshyn for New Pathway – Ukrainian News*

For the Ukrainian Canadian Social Services branch in Vancouver (UCSS-V), October and November 2022 brought a few highlights. The branch president, Roman Sawycky, received a YouTube video from his contacts at the Marhanets Hospital (Dnipro Oblast) showing that the truck of medical supplies UCSS-V helped load in August in Chilliwack, BC, had arrived safely, and that men and women were unloading the contents and carrying them into the hospital. While the video showed larger items being unloaded, we heard that nurses were taking “garbage bags” filled with clean, laundered bed sheets off the truck and racing back into the hospital with them. Even items like bed linens, which seem common and mundane to us, are in short supply in the war zone, and the people there are immensely grateful for whatever supplies they can receive: big or small, sophisticated or simple.

Then, on Saturday, October 22, at the Rotary World Help warehouse in Coquitlam, BC, UCSS-V helped load and seal another shipping container destined for Eastern Ukraine. CityNews Vancouver recorded some of the loading activity.

According to CityNews Vancouver, the October 22 load contained over $1 million of medical equipment and supplies, including a unique portable “C-arm” X-ray machine and a rack of specialized orthopedic supplies such as screws, plates, rods and fixators. The load also included X-ray monitors, ultrasound machines, hospital beds and wheelchairs.

While UCSS-V has been shipping primarily medical supplies, the October 2022 container also carried a unique cargo: approximately 500 donated, hand-sewn baby quilts, to be distributed to a number of orphanages in Ukraine.

In November, the loading continued. On November 4, UCSS-V loaded and sealed its 21st shipping container, which was also the sixth since the Russian invasion of Ukraine. While it included some equipment that didn’t fit into the October 22 container (for example, two “mini” C-arm X-ray machines), this one contained the first tranche of approximately 400 hospital beds and mattresses donated by Fraser Health (the BC health authority for the Fraser Valley, from Delta in the west to Mission and Hope in the east). As with the August loading, this one was done in conjunction with Rotary World Help and volunteers from the HERO Society (Canadian ex-military personnel).

UCSS-V planned to load and ship one or two more containers of hospital beds, mattresses and other donated equipment and supplies before the end of the year.

UCSS-V president Roman Sawycky reiterated that “… while the supplies and equipment are donated by health authorities, hospitals, clinics and individuals, the transportation costs are a cash outlay, now running as much as $15,000 per shipment from Vancouver to Halifax to Poland and then we face considerable difficulty and costs in transporting the supplies by truck to the hospitals and clinics to the war zone in eastern and southern Ukraine. All donations sent to Ukrainian Canadian Social Services – Vancouver will receive a tax receipt. Our address is #246-7610 Evans Road, Chilliwack, BC V2R 2Z5”.

